---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Contours from '../components/Contours.astro';
import Timeline from '../components/Timeline.astro';
import Skills from '../components/Skills.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
const projects = (await getCollection('projects')).sort((a,b)=>a.data.priority-b.data.priority);
---
<Layout title="Cascadia Field Notes" description="Akaash Bella is a computer science graduate researcher in Portland, Oregon, working across machine learning, software engineering, and human-centered design.">
  <section class="cover wrap" aria-labelledby="cover-title">
    <div class="cover-top meta"><span>Volume 01 / An ongoing inquiry</span><span>Pacific Northwest · Est. 2026</span></div>
    <div class="cover-grid">
      <div class="cover-copy"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="rust">00</span> / Computer science & human questions</p><h1 id="cover-title">Akaash<br /><span class="surname">Bella<span class="period">.</span></span></h1><p class="cover-tagline">Studying systems.<br /><em>Building for people.</em></p><p class="cover-description">Computer science graduate researcher working across machine learning, software engineering, and human-centered design.</p><div class="cover-actions"><a class="button" href="/work/">Explore the work <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="/research/">Read the research <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div>
      <figure class="cover-art"><div class="art-registration meta"><span>AB — 001</span><span>Contour study</span></div><Contours /><div class="art-coordinate"><span class="locator" aria-hidden="true">＋</span><p class="meta">Portland, Oregon<br /><span>45.52° N / 122.68° W</span></p></div><figcaption class="meta">Illustrative contours · not a geographic survey</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
    <div class="cover-bottom"><span class="meta">A field journal of research & engineering</span><a href="#introduction" class="meta">Begin reading <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a></div>
  </section>

  <section id="introduction" class="chapter intro wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">01 / A point of departure</p><div class="intro-grid"><h2>Between the technical<br />and the <em>human.</em></h2><div><p class="large-copy">My work moves between two questions: how technical systems behave, and how people actually use them.</p><p>I study computer science at Oregon State University, building on a background in computer science and innovation management. Today, that means investigating CNN weight perturbation, supporting students, and building tools with deliberate engineering choices.</p><a href="/about/" class="text-link">More about my background <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="selected" aria-labelledby="investigations-title">
    <div class="section-heading wrap"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / Selected investigations</p><h2 id="investigations-title">Three lines of inquiry.</h2></div><a class="text-link" href="/work/">All work <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div>
    <article class="research-feature"><div class="wrap research-feature-grid"><div class="feature-meta"><span class="meta">Investigation 01</span><span class="status-light">Research in progress</span></div><div class="research-feature-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Machine learning / Oregon State University</p><h3>What changes<br />when weights<br /><em>are uncertain?</em></h3><p>Controlled Gaussian noise. Four CNN architectures. One careful distinction between clean performance and weight robustness.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/weight-perturbation/">Inside the research <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div><figure class="research-plate"><div class="meta plate-label">Plate 01 / The perturbation model</div><div class="formula-plate">W′ <span>=</span> W + <i>α</i> · σ(W) · ε</div><div class="noise-plate" aria-hidden="true">{Array.from({length:80},(_,i)=><span style={`opacity:${0.22+(Math.sin(i*7.1)+1)*0.34};transform:translateY(${Math.sin(i*2.7)*12}px)`}></span>)}</div><figcaption>Temporary noise in the forward pass.<br />Clean weights remain the source of truth.</figcaption><p class="meta plate-note">Conceptual diagram · no measured results</p></figure><div class="research-feature-foot meta"><span>PyTorch / TorchVision / Slurm</span><span>240 planned training runs · runtime verification pending</span></div></div></article>
    <div class="wrap secondary-features">
      <article class="seek-feature"><div class="feature-number meta">Investigation 02 / Software architecture</div><figure class="note-index"><div class="meta">ResearchSeek / Proposed index</div><div class="index-line"><span>01</span><span>Notes, kept close.</span><span>↗</span></div><div class="index-line"><span>02</span><span>Meaning, made searchable.</span><span>↗</span></div><div class="index-line"><span>03</span><span>Sources, always attached.</span><span>↗</span></div><figcaption>Local notes → SQLite → C++ retrieval<br />Planned architecture · Stage 1 foundation</figcaption></figure><h3>ResearchSeek</h3><p>A local-first search project at the boundary of Python, C++, and the research notebook. An architecture designed to be measured.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/researchseek/">Read the engineering notes <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></article>
      <article class="agbiz-feature"><div class="feature-number meta">Investigation 03 / Interdisciplinary design</div><h3>Better tools start<br />with <em>better questions.</em></h3><p>Leading an Oregon State capstone team to explore usability, conversational assistance, and trust in agricultural decision support.</p><a class="text-link" href="/work/agbizlogic/">The AgBizLogic case study <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a><figure class="poster-crop"><a href="/work/agbizlogic/" aria-label="View the AgBizLogic case study and Expo poster"><img src="/images/agbiz-poster.webp" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" alt="AgBizLogic team Expo poster outlining the capstone research, process, and recommendations" /></a><figcaption>Oregon State Capstone Expo, 2025 · Team research</figcaption></figure></article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" aria-labelledby="additional-title"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">03 / Along the way</p><h2 id="additional-title">Other fieldwork.</h2></div><p class="caption">Applications, language tools,<br />and the business of technology.</p></div><div class="project-index">{projects.filter(p=>p.data.priority>3).map((project,i)=><a class="project-row" href={`/work/${project.id}/`}><span class="meta">0{i+4}</span><h3>{project.data.title}</h3><span class="project-row-description">{project.data.summary}</span><span class="meta">{project.data.period}</span><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>)}</div></section>

  <section class="chapter wrap timeline-section"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">04 / A working timeline</p><h2>Learning, in layers.</h2></div><a href="/resume/" class="text-link">Full resume <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><Timeline /></section>
  <section class="chapter wrap methods-section"><div><p class="eyebrow">05 / Methods & tools</p><h2>A practical<br /><em>working vocabulary.</em></h2><p class="caption">Tools follow the question.<br />Clarity comes first.</p></div><Skills /></section>
  <section class="closing wrap"><p class="eyebrow">06 / A closing note</p><h2>Good work begins<br />with a <em>conversation.</em></h2><div><p>I’m interested in thoughtful engineering, research questions worth testing, and tools that make sense to the people using them.</p><a class="button" href="/contact/">Get in touch <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="/resume/">Read my resume <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div></section>
</Layout>
